;; Reviewed 2026-10-09 by the user (written by Claude, from the
;; design's sections 4, 7 and 14 and a run on the VAX). Why this is
;; right:
;; - Under -OC (section 14, decided 2026-10-09) SHORTINT is a word
;;   (.BLKW, MOVW), INTEGER a longword (.BLKL, MOVL) and LONGINT a
;;   quadword (.BLKQ), aligned at most to a longword (section 4).
;; - A LONGINT is lowered as a HUGEINT is under -O2: a constant as two
;;   MOVLs, low longword first; + as ADDL2 and ADWC after the INTEGER is
;;   widened (ASHL #-31 for its high longword); a comparison high
;;   longwords first, signed, then low ones unsigned; DIV a call of
;;   POC_HDIV with both operands by address, the quotient in R0/R1.
;; - Twice's LONGINT value parameter comes by address and is copied to
;;   the frame on entry (MOVQ @4(AP)); its result is R0/R1. The caller
;;   passes a copy in its frame (MOVAQ -16(FP)).
;; - A LONGINT index is checked on both halves (TSTL of the high, then
;;   CMPL/BLEQU of the low against 3) before the element's 8 bytes are
;;   scaled by MULL2.
;; - ORD of a CHAR is MOVZBL and ORD of a SET a MOVL, INTEGER being a
;;   longword; LONG of a SHORTINT is CVTWL; SHORT of a LONGINT keeps the
;;   low longword; ASH of a LONGINT is ASHQ.
;; - SYS$EXIT's INTEGER parameter, a longword, goes by value.
;; - Run on the VAX (guest-run.com): every check holds, exit status
;;   %X00000FF1.
